{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We have CNF with 12 variables. Clauses list:\n\n1. [5,12] => (x5 ∨ x12)\n2. [10,3] => (x10 ∨ x3)\n3. [6,8] => (x6 ∨ x8)\n4. [-12,4] => (¬x12 ∨ x4)\n5. [-6,11] => (¬x6 ∨ x11)\n6. [-8,11] => (¬x8 ∨ x11)\n7. [2,7] => (x2 ∨ x7)\n8. [-3,1] => (¬x3 ∨ x1)\n9. [-10,1] => (¬x10 ∨ x1)\n10. [-7,9] => (¬x7 ∨ x9)\n11. [-2,9] => (¬x2 ∨ x9)\n12. [-5,4] => (¬x5 ∨ x4)\n\nWe need count of satisfying assignments of 12 boolean variables.\n\nWe can attempt to analyze constraints. Let's denote variables: x1..x12.\n\nClauses pairwise. Let's try to deduce implications.\n\nClause 1: x5 ∨ x12. So at least one of x5, x12 true.\n\nClause 4: ¬x12 ∨ x4 => x12 → x4. So if x12 true, x4 true. Also if x4 false, then x12 false.\n\nClause 12: ¬x5 ∨ x4 => x5 → x4. So if x5 true, x4 true. Also if x4 false, then x5 false.\n\nThus if either x5 or x12 true, x4 must be true. So x4 true unless both x5 and x12 false. But clause 1 requires at least one true, so x4 must be true always. Wait: clause 1 ensures at least one of x5 or x12 true. That implies x4 true due to implications. So x4 is forced true.\n\nThus x4 = 1.\n\nNow clause 1 satisfied regardless of x5/x12 as long as at least one true. So we need to count assignments of x5,x12 with at least one true: 3 possibilities: (1,0),(0,1),(1,1). So 3 combos.\n\nNow other clauses involve other variables.\n\nClause 2: x10 ∨ x3. So at least one of x10,x3 true.\n\nClause 8: ¬x3 ∨ x1 => x3 → x1. So if x3 true, x1 true. If x1 false, x3 false.\n\nClause 9: ¬x10 ∨ x1 => x10 → x1. So if x10 true, x1 true. If x1 false, x10 false.\n\nThus if either x3 or x10 true, x1 must be true. So x1 is true unless both x3 and x10 false. But clause 2 requires at least one true, so x1 must be true. So x1 = 1.\n\nThus x1 forced true.\n\nNow clause 3: x6 ∨ x8. So at least one of x6,x8 true.\n\nClause 5: ¬x6 ∨ x11 => x6 → x11. So if x6 true, x11 true.\n\nClause 6: ¬x8 ∨ x11 => x8 →